# Working on [[.Name]]

Read README.md, CONTRIBUTING.md and native CI before editing. Use Go 1.27.3,
GOWORK=off and published dependencies without sibling replacements.

- This is an application-owned engineering skeleton, not the Starter examples.
- Add real contexts and services on demand. Keep one module for both topologies.
- Use nostosx add api for explicit v1 RPC/message/binding/adapter scaffolds, then
  make generate and go mod tidy. Implement the unimplemented method and its tests;
  authorization, CQRS use cases and HTTP mounting remain application-owned.
- Omit both HTTP flags for gRPC-only APIs. For existing Proto contracts, use
  nostosx api scaffold to fill missing unary methods offline and preserve matching
  implementations. Add behavior tests when implementing those methods.
- nostosx doctor only reads project files and trusted tool versions; it neither
  installs tools nor replaces code and contract checks.
- Keep application/domain independent of protobuf and gRPC; map at port/rpc.
- Commands may write; queries only read. Define narrow ports beside real use cases
  and stable CQRS operation metadata; compose middleware in module.go when needed.
- Bootstrap owns transport lifecycle; service-private adapters stay internal.
- Keep loopback defaults and authenticated gRPC. New HTTP routes need explicit
  authentication, authorization, input bounds and safe error mapping.
- Agent tools and authorization are explicitly supplied. Model endpoints are
  trusted startup configuration, never user input. Preserve runner limits.
- Proto sources and HTTP options are authoritative; regenerate explicitly with
  the pinned CLI and Buf, tidy dependencies, then check drift. Do not edit generated
  files or copy generator source into the application. Normal builds need no CLI.
- Preserve .nostosx.lock ownership and failed-plugin isolation. Do not add project
  metadata files, fake business examples, empty placeholders or archtest packages.
- Run make check, lint, vulncheck, api-check and smoke for relevant changes.
- Never commit, push, tag or deploy without explicit user authorization.
